"""The generator of Q++: a Q++ 0.2 source to Lean, and the map from the Lean lines back to the Q++ lines.

The Lean of a source is its Mathlib reading. Each conclusion row of an item is a statement `NAME.statement : Prop`
(NAME: the label and the key, `lem_x_1`) over Mathlib's \u211a and \u2115 and, when its proof line generates, the theorem
`NAME : NAME.statement`. A mass (`QQ>0`) or a count (`NN>=1`) is a rational or a natural number with its bound as a
hypothesis `hp_x`; a given declares the binders, and its relations are the hypotheses `h_1`, `h_2`, ...; a row
`c := t` of a given names a term: a binder `c` and its definition `h_c : c = t`. A cited fact (`cit`) is a statement,
never an axiom: a result whose proof cites it takes it as a hypothesis, and a row `mathlib: NAME (MODULE)` proves it
from the Mathlib lemma NAME. A row with a hole, or outside the fragment, is listed with the reason in the header of the
file. qpp/README.md lists the proof words and the Lean that each one writes.

The kernel target (qpp/modlean.py for the modules, `--kernel-target [NS]` for a source): each row is stated
again over the words of the kernel of Q++ (qpp/lean/QppKernel/Words.lean: counts and masses over Lean core, zero
absent), and that statement is proved from its Mathlib reading through the bridge (lean/QppBridge, simp set
qpp_bridge). The Mathlib reading is in the namespace QppMathlib (QppMathlib.NS), the statements over the kernel in
Qpp.Modules (Qpp.NS); a row that the kernel printer does not write yet is reported with its reason.

Linear programs. An item whose given declares scales in `QQ>=0` is a program: its rows are the hypotheses of the
given on the scales (not their guards), in order; the other hypotheses bound its parameters. `LP[p_1, ..., p_J]`,
the LP certificate (weak duality), proves a row `cost >= value`: the linear combination `p_1 * h_r1 + ... +
p_J * h_rJ` of the rows, a price 0 leaving its row out; after `;` further data of the same line (a reduced cost whose
sign a hypothesis on the parameters gives, `c * h_k * h_g`). A row `at: x := t, ...; x := t', ...` of the item gives
points: it states that each satisfies the rows (keeping the given on the parameters) and that the cost there is the
value of the first row `cost >= value`; its proof is generated (`V` per row, `I` for the cost). qpp/lp.py checks the
same certificates in exact arithmetic.

`--check` writes the file and compiles it in the Lake project lean/ (OUT must lie in a library of lean/, e.g.
QppExamples/): Lake builds its imports, `lake env lean` compiles it, and each Lean error is printed at its Q++ line.

With an `advertise` line and the kernel target, the source is also a Palomar entry (kernel_entry; the entry of the
Lake project lean/ is qpp/examples/means.qpp): the advertised statements over the kernel words and the cited facts
they take go to OUT/Statements.lean (Lean core); the Challenge, OUT/Challenge.lean, repeats QppKernel/Base.lean,
QppKernel/Words.lean and the statements verbatim, then states the advertised theorems with `sorry` (Lean core only);
OUT/Solution.lean imports OUT.lean, which proves each statement from its Mathlib reading through the bridge;
--comparator writes comparator.json.

OUT past the size of a part (qpp/modlean.py, PART bytes or LINES lines) is written in parts PartK.lean in the folder of
its name, each importing the one before, and OUT imports the last. `--audit PATH` writes the axiom audit of OUT, one
`#print axioms` per theorem. `--refused` reads a source of cases
that must be refused (qpp/examples/refused/): it fails if one of its rows is generated, and OUT lists the reason of
each refusal, so that CI, which compares the generated files, sees a reason that changes; `--given LABEL,...` names
the items that set up the cases (cited rules they apply), which must be generated instead.

Positivity: a source is positive unless it carries the mark `application: REASON` (data of both signs).
In a positive source a row with a form of both signs (QQ, ZZ, a - b outside the deficit
max(a - b, 0), -a, |a|, or a vocabulary name whose line has one) is refused, and a vocabulary line with one is an
error; `--allow ID,...` lists the rows and lines it may keep (LABEL(KEY), LABEL(given), `HEAD NAME`), as
modules/both_signs.toml does for a module (qpp/modlean.py). With `--refused` a vocabulary line that is refused (for its
signs or any other reason) is reported with its reason and the source goes on, and every vocabulary line that
--allow does not list must be refused: the lines it lists set up the cases.

Usage: python3 -m qpp.kernel SRC.qpp -o OUT.lean [--module NAME] [--kernel-target [NS]] [--audit PATH]
       [--comparator PATH] [--refused [--given LABEL,...]] [--allow ID,...] [--check]
       python3 -m qpp.kernel --primitives (the list of the primitives over Mathlib, as qpp/README.md has it)
"""
